1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,040
Your rent is $1,100.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:04,519
You have three days before your landlord kicks you out

3
00:00:04,519 --> 00:00:06,160
and your bank account is empty.

4
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,440
What do you do?

5
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,519
In October 2007, that wasn't a hypothetical question

6
00:00:10,519 --> 00:00:13,480
for Brian Chesky and his roommate, Joe Gebier.

7
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:15,359
They were two broke design school graduates

8
00:00:15,359 --> 00:00:17,760
sharing an apartment, completely unemployed

9
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:19,359
and scrambling for any quick cash

10
00:00:19,359 --> 00:00:21,039
just to survive the weekend.

11
00:00:21,039 --> 00:00:23,480
Then pure luck handed them an opening.

12
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,920
A massive international design conference was flooding

13
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,359
into town and every single hotel room in the city

14
00:00:28,359 --> 00:00:30,160
was completely sold out.

15
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:32,679
Tourists had nowhere to sleep, so Brian and Joe

16
00:00:32,679 --> 00:00:35,000
looked at their empty floor, ran to target,

17
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
bought three cheap blue air mattresses

18
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,240
and charged desperate designers 80 bucks a night

19
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,920
to sleep on their rug, throwing in a box of pop tarts

20
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:42,960
in the morning.

21
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,719
18 years later, that embarrassing emergency hustle

22
00:00:45,719 --> 00:00:48,679
on the floor became a $100 billion empire.

23
00:00:48,679 --> 00:00:50,840
And if you are an entrepreneur or solo builder

24
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:53,359
trying to start something with zero cash today,

25
00:00:53,359 --> 00:00:55,759
this journey holds the ultimate blueprint.

26
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:57,240
Because by the end of this video,

27
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:59,880
you are going to see the three uncensored tactical survival

28
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,600
moves Brian used to hijack an entire industry

29
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:04,719
and the fatal scaling trap you must avoid

30
00:01:04,719 --> 00:01:07,719
so your business doesn't destroy itself once it wins.

31
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:10,200
That first weekend actually worked.

32
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,359
The three designers handed over $240 in cash

33
00:01:13,359 --> 00:01:15,079
and the rent was paid.

34
00:01:15,079 --> 00:01:17,480
Brian and Joe looked at the deflated air mattresses

35
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:21,000
on the floor and realized, wait, if complete strangers

36
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,760
are willing to sleep in our living room,

37
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,560
what if people could do this in every city on earth?

38
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:27,079
There was only one problem.

39
00:01:27,079 --> 00:01:29,319
Neither of them knew how to write software code.

40
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:31,519
They were industrial designers who drew sketches,

41
00:01:31,519 --> 00:01:32,799
not programmers.

42
00:01:32,799 --> 00:01:35,680
So they called up their former roommate Nate Blasciac,

43
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,000
a gifted software engineer, and convinced him

44
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,280
to build a primitive website called Air Bed and Breakfast.

45
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,519
The idea was simple, turn everyday homes

46
00:01:43,519 --> 00:01:45,439
into mini bed and breakfasts.

47
00:01:45,439 --> 00:01:48,079
Once the website was live in early 2008,

48
00:01:48,079 --> 00:01:50,200
they thought the hardest part was over.

49
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,239
They needed cash to keep the servers running

50
00:01:52,239 --> 00:01:54,000
so they took their brand new website

51
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,480
to top Silicon Valley venture capitalists.

52
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:58,680
The meetings were an absolute disaster.

53
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,000
Investors literally laughed in their faces,

54
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,480
renowned venture capitalists,

55
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,560
walked out of coffee shops or stared at them

56
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,480
like they were completely insane.

57
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:09,439
Why on earth would anyone invest in an app for strangers

58
00:02:09,439 --> 00:02:12,159
to sleep on air mattresses on dirty floors?

59
00:02:12,159 --> 00:02:14,199
Every single investor said no.

60
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:16,680
Zero funding and zero users.

61
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,800
Over the next 10 months, the business completely flatlined.

62
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,560
At the South Byte Southwest Festival,

63
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:23,639
they got exactly two bookings

64
00:02:23,639 --> 00:02:25,319
and one of them was Brian himself.

65
00:02:25,319 --> 00:02:27,840
Now, the three founders were completely broke

66
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:29,199
and drowning in death.

67
00:02:29,199 --> 00:02:31,280
Not one credit card, not two cards,

68
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,159
but entire baseball card binder albums

69
00:02:33,159 --> 00:02:34,960
filled with maxed out plastic.

70
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:38,520
$20,000 in high interest debt, no revenue coming in,

71
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:41,319
and collection agencies calling them every single afternoon.

72
00:02:41,319 --> 00:02:43,000
How do you keep a website alive

73
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,560
when you can't even afford groceries?

74
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,719
Then came August 2008,

75
00:02:46,719 --> 00:02:49,159
the Democratic National Convention in Denver.

76
00:02:49,159 --> 00:02:51,120
A hundred thousand people were showing up,

77
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:55,240
and there were only 17,000 hotel rooms in the entire city.

78
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:57,560
It was the exact same hotel crisis

79
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:00,360
that saved them the first time, but 50 times bigger.

80
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:03,439
The founders had zero cash left to keep their servers online.

81
00:03:03,439 --> 00:03:06,159
So they did what any rational tech founder does.

82
00:03:06,159 --> 00:03:08,120
They went into the breakfast cereal business.

83
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:10,319
They convinced a college student in Rhode Island

84
00:03:10,319 --> 00:03:13,360
to print 1,000 custom cardboard boxes on credit.

85
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:15,159
They folded them by hand in their kitchen,

86
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:16,840
glued them with hot glue guns,

87
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:18,439
and filled them with cheap bulk cereal

88
00:03:18,439 --> 00:03:21,159
called Obama O's and Captain McCain's.

89
00:03:21,159 --> 00:03:25,120
At $40 a box, people thought it was a brilliant political stunt,

90
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:26,319
but it wasn't a stunt at all.

91
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:27,879
It was pure grocery money.

92
00:03:27,879 --> 00:03:30,360
They sold $30,000 worth of cereal,

93
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:32,039
paid off their credit card interest,

94
00:03:32,039 --> 00:03:34,639
and bought themselves four more months of life.

95
00:03:34,639 --> 00:03:38,159
The $30,000 from cereal bought them four months of runway,

96
00:03:38,159 --> 00:03:40,280
but their website was still flatlined.

97
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,919
In January 2009, they walked into an interview

98
00:03:42,919 --> 00:03:45,919
at Y Combinator, the most prestigious startup accelerator

99
00:03:45,919 --> 00:03:47,039
in Silicon Valley.

100
00:03:47,039 --> 00:03:49,759
The interview with founder Paul Graham was a disaster.

101
00:03:49,759 --> 00:03:52,120
Graham listened to the pitch about random strangers

102
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:54,960
sleeping on air mattresses in private apartments,

103
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:55,840
and hated it.

104
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,879
He told him flat out, the idea is terrible.

105
00:03:58,879 --> 00:04:00,000
People gonna get robbed,

106
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,360
people are gonna get murdered,

107
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:03,400
and nobody will ever pay for this.

108
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:04,960
As they were packing up to leave,

109
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:06,599
Brian reached into his backpack,

110
00:04:06,599 --> 00:04:09,360
pulled out an authentic box of Obama O's cereal,

111
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:10,840
and handed it across the table.

112
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:13,520
He explained that when venture capitalists laughed at them

113
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,439
and their bank accounts hit zero,

114
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:18,879
they hand folded and glued 1,000 boxes in their kitchen,

115
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:20,120
just to keep the lights on.

116
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,399
Paul Graham looked down at the cereal box,

117
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:24,939
looked up at the three exhausted founders.

118
00:04:24,939 --> 00:04:27,060
And said something that changed startup history.

119
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:30,980
If you can convince people to pay $40 for a $4 box of cereal,

120
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:33,500
you can convince people to sleep on air beds.

121
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,420
You're in, they got $20,000 in seed funding,

122
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:39,620
but getting into Y Combinator didn't magically save them.

123
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:41,220
Right here in March 2009,

124
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:44,220
they made the smartest strategic pivot of their lives.

125
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:46,699
The original name, air bed and breakfast,

126
00:04:46,699 --> 00:04:48,939
was actively suffocating the business.

127
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:50,740
Travelers assumed they would be sleeping

128
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:52,660
on squeaky rubber pole mattresses

129
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:54,420
in a stranger's living room.

130
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:56,660
And hosts with beautiful spare bedrooms

131
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:59,339
or empty apartments refused to list on a site

132
00:04:59,339 --> 00:05:01,980
that literally had the word air bed in the title.

133
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:03,459
So in the middle of Y Combinator,

134
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:05,060
they officially bought the domain

135
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:07,420
and shortened the name down to just three letters.

136
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:09,139
Airbnb.com.

137
00:05:09,139 --> 00:05:11,500
Dropping the words air bed and breakfast

138
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:13,459
was the psychological masterstroke

139
00:05:13,459 --> 00:05:14,980
that transformed them from an eccentric,

140
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:18,459
florental gimmick into a real marketplace for actual homes.

141
00:05:18,459 --> 00:05:21,339
Yet even with the new name, growth refused a bunch.

142
00:05:21,339 --> 00:05:24,500
By June 2009, the accelerator was ending

143
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:28,019
and their weekly revenue was stuck at barely $200 a week.

144
00:05:28,019 --> 00:05:30,139
They were living in a windowless office

145
00:05:30,139 --> 00:05:32,100
surviving on instant noodles

146
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,860
and staring at a growth chart as flat as a dead man's pulse.

147
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:37,899
Paul Graham pulled Brian and Joe aside

148
00:05:37,899 --> 00:05:39,339
and asked a simple question,

149
00:05:39,339 --> 00:05:41,300
where are your few actual customers?

150
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:44,180
Brian answered that 30 of their 40 active listings

151
00:05:44,180 --> 00:05:45,300
were in New York City.

152
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:48,060
That was when Graham gave them the single piece of advice

153
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:49,779
that became Silicon Valley law.

154
00:05:49,779 --> 00:05:51,060
Do things that don't scale.

155
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:52,579
Get on a plane to New York right now.

156
00:05:52,579 --> 00:05:54,779
Go knock on every single host door.

157
00:05:54,779 --> 00:05:57,620
Brian and Joe didn't have money for playing tickets.

158
00:05:57,620 --> 00:05:59,220
So they put them on another credit card.

159
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:02,100
They flew to Brooklyn, rented a $500 camera

160
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,779
and literally walked down the streets

161
00:06:03,779 --> 00:06:05,540
knocking on their users' doors.

162
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:06,819
They didn't try to sell anything.

163
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:09,459
They asked hosts if they could take professional photos

164
00:06:09,459 --> 00:06:10,819
of their apartments for free.

165
00:06:10,819 --> 00:06:13,300
They sat in their kitchens, drank tea with them

166
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,379
and asked what sucked about using the website.

167
00:06:15,379 --> 00:06:18,060
And right there, in a tiny apartment in New York,

168
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:20,259
the founders discovered the dirty secret

169
00:06:20,259 --> 00:06:22,259
behind why their company was failing.

170
00:06:22,259 --> 00:06:24,259
Their website didn't have a tech problem.

171
00:06:24,259 --> 00:06:25,740
It had a visual problem.

172
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:27,459
Every listing on the site was shot

173
00:06:27,459 --> 00:06:29,779
on cheap 2008 flip phones.

174
00:06:29,779 --> 00:06:33,100
The rooms were dark, blurry and looked like crime scenes.

175
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:34,699
Nobody wanted to stay in a room

176
00:06:34,699 --> 00:06:36,579
that looked like a basement hostage video.

177
00:06:36,579 --> 00:06:38,420
Brian took professional, bright,

178
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:41,500
wide-angle interior photos, uploaded them that evening.

179
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,339
And within 30 days, bookings in New York doubled.

180
00:06:44,339 --> 00:06:45,220
Then they tripled.

181
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:46,819
For the first time in two years,

182
00:06:46,819 --> 00:06:48,660
revenue started rocketing up.

183
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:50,060
The New York photography hack

184
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:52,300
proved people actually wanted to book unique homes,

185
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:53,740
but now they hit a new wall.

186
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:56,500
They had no money to market the company nationwide.

187
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,980
They couldn't afford expensive Google search ads

188
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:01,459
and billboard companies wouldn't even return their calls.

189
00:07:01,459 --> 00:07:02,899
So the founders looked at the biggest

190
00:07:02,899 --> 00:07:05,939
online real estate classified site on a planet.

191
00:07:05,939 --> 00:07:09,660
Craigslist, in 2010, Craigslist had over 50 million

192
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:12,939
active visitors looking for housing every single month.

193
00:07:12,939 --> 00:07:14,980
But they had zero public API.

194
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:17,139
There was no official way to plug another company

195
00:07:17,139 --> 00:07:18,139
into their system.

196
00:07:18,139 --> 00:07:20,379
So Airbnb's technical team pulled off

197
00:07:20,379 --> 00:07:23,699
one of the most audacious, legally gray growth hacks

198
00:07:23,699 --> 00:07:24,819
in internet history.

199
00:07:24,819 --> 00:07:27,980
They reverse engineered Craigslist form submissions.

200
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:30,300
Whenever a host created a listing on Airbnb,

201
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:32,220
a simple checkbox popped up.

202
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:34,699
Would you also like to post this listing to Craigslist?

203
00:07:34,699 --> 00:07:36,100
If the host clicked yes,

204
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:38,779
a custom script automatically formatted the listing,

205
00:07:38,779 --> 00:07:41,259
bypass Craigslist's anti-spam filters,

206
00:07:41,259 --> 00:07:44,180
published it to millions of Craigslist department hunters,

207
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:46,620
and inserted a clean, clickable link reading.

208
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:50,060
Check availability and book securely on Airbnb.

209
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:50,939
It was brilliant.

210
00:07:50,939 --> 00:07:52,980
It was completely unauthorized,

211
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:55,660
and it siphoned millions of high intent travelers.

212
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:58,699
Directly off Craigslist servers for exactly $0

213
00:07:58,699 --> 00:07:59,899
in marketing spend.

214
00:07:59,899 --> 00:08:03,300
By the time Craigslist realized their classified section

215
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:05,899
was being used as a free customer acquisition funnel

216
00:08:05,899 --> 00:08:08,060
and shut it down, Airbnb was already

217
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:10,220
an unstoppable global juggernaut.

218
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:13,579
Over the next decade, from 2011 to 2019,

219
00:08:13,579 --> 00:08:15,180
nothing could slow them down.

220
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,459
Venture capital giants like Sequoia poured

221
00:08:17,459 --> 00:08:19,660
in hundreds of millions of dollars.

222
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:22,779
The platform exploded from an air bed in San Francisco

223
00:08:22,779 --> 00:08:26,420
to seven million listings across 80,000 cities worldwide.

224
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:29,459
Traditional hotel chains with their sterile lobbies,

225
00:08:29,459 --> 00:08:32,259
expensive room service, and stiff front desks

226
00:08:32,259 --> 00:08:33,419
were getting slaughtered.

227
00:08:33,419 --> 00:08:36,500
By two design grads who let people stay in private villas,

228
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:39,019
rustic tree houses, and beach front-offs,

229
00:08:39,019 --> 00:08:40,740
the founders had conquered the world.

230
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:42,860
And then March 2020 arrived.

231
00:08:42,860 --> 00:08:46,659
In literally eight weeks, the entire global travel industry

232
00:08:46,659 --> 00:08:48,779
vanished off the face of the earth.

233
00:08:48,779 --> 00:08:51,580
Airbnb's bookings collapsed by 80%.

234
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:54,620
The company was forced to issue over $1 billion

235
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:58,340
in cash refunds to furious travelers out of its own reserves.

236
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:02,460
On May 5, 2020, Brian Cheskey had a hold back tears

237
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:04,019
on a company wide video call.

238
00:09:04,019 --> 00:09:08,299
And lay off 1900 people, 25% of his workforce.

239
00:09:08,299 --> 00:09:11,179
Wall Street analysts declared the company dead in the water.

240
00:09:11,179 --> 00:09:13,899
Most founders would have declared bankruptcy right there.

241
00:09:13,899 --> 00:09:16,139
Instead, Cheskey stripped the company down

242
00:09:16,139 --> 00:09:17,259
to its bare metal.

243
00:09:17,259 --> 00:09:19,700
He killed expensive moonshot divisions,

244
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:21,259
slash corporate overhead.

245
00:09:21,259 --> 00:09:23,940
And notice something fascinating in the booking data.

246
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:26,340
People were terrified of getting on airplanes

247
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,820
to fly to crowded European capitals.

248
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:30,019
But they were desperate to get out

249
00:09:30,019 --> 00:09:31,779
of their cramped city apartments.

250
00:09:31,779 --> 00:09:34,539
Millions of remote workers were packing their cars

251
00:09:34,539 --> 00:09:36,820
and driving two hours into the mountains

252
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:40,500
to rent secluded cabins with fast Wi-Fi for months at a time.

253
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:42,220
The stocation boom was born.

254
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:45,179
By the end of the year, Airbnb wasn't just surviving.

255
00:09:45,179 --> 00:09:49,100
It was posting record cash flow on December 10, 2020,

256
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:51,139
right in the middle of a global crisis.

257
00:09:51,139 --> 00:09:53,460
Airbnb pulled off one of the most miraculous

258
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:56,139
Wall Street debuts in financial history.

259
00:09:56,139 --> 00:09:57,779
The stock opened on the NASDAQ,

260
00:09:57,779 --> 00:09:59,779
doubled on its very first day of trading,

261
00:09:59,779 --> 00:10:03,500
and valued the company at over $100 billion.

262
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,299
The broke design school graduate,

263
00:10:05,299 --> 00:10:08,460
who is three days from an eviction notice in 2007,

264
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:11,779
was now running the most valuable travel company on the planet.

265
00:10:11,779 --> 00:10:14,940
This is where the story stops being an inspirational poster

266
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:18,259
for young entrepreneurs and turns into a corporate horror movie.

267
00:10:18,259 --> 00:10:21,059
Because when a company reaches $100 billion,

268
00:10:21,059 --> 00:10:23,940
the original incentives warp beyond recognition.

269
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:26,299
The everyday people who rented a spare room

270
00:10:26,299 --> 00:10:29,019
to pay their rent or meet travelers from overseas.

271
00:10:29,019 --> 00:10:30,620
They got completely pushed out.

272
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:33,179
Instead, Wall Street private equity funds

273
00:10:33,179 --> 00:10:35,100
and predatory landlords realized

274
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:37,460
they could buy up entire apartment buildings.

275
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:40,220
Kick out local families and run unlicensed

276
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:42,179
commercial shadow hotels.

277
00:10:42,179 --> 00:10:46,419
By 2022 and 2023, you would browse Airbnb,

278
00:10:46,419 --> 00:10:49,740
find a nice apartment listed for $150 a night,

279
00:10:49,740 --> 00:10:50,659
and click checkout.

280
00:10:50,659 --> 00:10:53,860
Suddenly, the bill exploded to $400.

281
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:57,899
$70 in platform service fees, $40 in occupancy taxes,

282
00:10:57,899 --> 00:11:00,259
and a staggering $180 cleaning fee.

283
00:11:00,259 --> 00:11:01,539
In the ultimate punchline,

284
00:11:01,539 --> 00:11:04,179
you unlock the front door after an eight-hour flight,

285
00:11:04,179 --> 00:11:07,220
and sitting on the kitchen counter was a laminated chore list.

286
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:09,740
Strip all the bed sheets, wash the linens,

287
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:11,779
run the dishwasher, wipe down the baseboards,

288
00:11:11,779 --> 00:11:14,340
take out the trash, and drive your garbage bags

289
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,779
four miles away to the local municipal dump.

290
00:11:16,779 --> 00:11:18,419
Guess looked at the $400 bill,

291
00:11:18,419 --> 00:11:20,259
looked at the mop in their hands,

292
00:11:20,259 --> 00:11:22,100
and realized the absurd truth.

293
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:24,220
They were paying luxury hotel prices

294
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:27,659
to do unpaid manual labor for an absentee landlord

295
00:11:27,659 --> 00:11:29,539
who wasn't even in the same state.

296
00:11:29,539 --> 00:11:31,139
Social media erupted.

297
00:11:31,139 --> 00:11:33,379
Millions of viral TikToks and tweets

298
00:11:33,379 --> 00:11:35,740
started posting the exact same headline.

299
00:11:35,740 --> 00:11:39,100
Hotels won because a hotel never hands you a mop,

300
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:40,899
or charges you a hidden cleaning fee,

301
00:11:40,899 --> 00:11:42,059
just to sleep in their bed.

302
00:11:42,059 --> 00:11:43,860
Then came the government guillotine,

303
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:45,740
communities blamed short-term rentals

304
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:47,220
for destroying neighborhoods,

305
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:49,500
and pricing local workers out of housing.

306
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:52,899
In September 2023, New York City dropped the hammer

307
00:11:52,899 --> 00:11:54,580
with local lie-18,

308
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:57,700
making unhosted short-term rentals virtually illegal,

309
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:02,379
and wiping out over 80% of Airbnb's New York listings overnight.

310
00:12:02,379 --> 00:12:04,379
Barcelona went even further,

311
00:12:04,379 --> 00:12:06,500
announcing that by November, 2028,

312
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,980
all 10,000 tourist departments in the city

313
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,620
will be completely wiped out.

314
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:12,299
Cities from Florence to Berlin,

315
00:12:12,299 --> 00:12:15,860
slammed down aggressive 90-day caps and zoning bands.

316
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:17,700
The company that started because a kid

317
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:20,259
couldn't afford his rent in 2007,

318
00:12:20,259 --> 00:12:22,659
was now accused of making rent unaffordable

319
00:12:22,659 --> 00:12:24,379
for an entire generation.

320
00:12:24,379 --> 00:12:25,700
That brings us to right now.

321
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:28,700
Airbnb is still a massive financial machine,

322
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:31,539
pulling in over $12 billion in annual revenue,

323
00:12:31,539 --> 00:12:33,820
two and a half billion in net profit,

324
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:37,379
and maintaining over 8 million active listings worldwide.

325
00:12:37,379 --> 00:12:40,659
But the explosive hyper-growth era is officially dead.

326
00:12:40,659 --> 00:12:42,139
Looking growth in mature markets

327
00:12:42,139 --> 00:12:44,100
has cooled to the single digits,

328
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:46,179
and the brand spent three straight years,

329
00:12:46,179 --> 00:12:48,500
getting roasted on every corner of the internet.

330
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:52,500
So how is Brian Chesky responding in 2025 and 2026?

331
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:56,059
By doing the one thing no proud founder ever wants to admit,

332
00:12:56,059 --> 00:12:58,379
he is desperately fixing his own machine.

333
00:12:58,379 --> 00:13:00,860
Airbnb rolled out total price display,

334
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:03,779
so hidden cleaning fees can no longer be ambushed to check out.

335
00:13:03,779 --> 00:13:06,820
They banned hosts from demanding unreasonable chore lists,

336
00:13:06,820 --> 00:13:08,419
like vacuuming and laundry,

337
00:13:08,419 --> 00:13:11,500
and in the ultimate comedic irony in the history of tech.

338
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:15,899
Airbnb's fastest growing category today is boutique hotels.

339
00:13:15,899 --> 00:13:18,019
The very industry the founders set out

340
00:13:18,019 --> 00:13:20,019
to destroy 18 years ago,

341
00:13:20,019 --> 00:13:22,820
with three cheap rubber mattresses on a living room floor,

342
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:24,820
is now the exact thing they're relying on

343
00:13:24,820 --> 00:13:26,820
to keep their business alive.

344
00:13:26,820 --> 00:13:29,179
So what is the real actionable playbook?

345
00:13:29,179 --> 00:13:30,779
You take from this 18-year war,

346
00:13:30,779 --> 00:13:33,019
if you strip away the billion dollar headlines,

347
00:13:33,019 --> 00:13:36,620
and look at how this machine was actually constructed from zero.

348
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:40,779
Three brutal rules emerge that every entrepreneur must memorize.

349
00:13:40,779 --> 00:13:43,659
First, borrow your distribution until you own it.

350
00:13:43,659 --> 00:13:46,820
Brian and Joe didn't spend a dime on Facebook or Google Ads.

351
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:50,419
They hijacked Craigslist 50 million users with a rogue script,

352
00:13:50,419 --> 00:13:52,259
and they turned a sold out conference

353
00:13:52,259 --> 00:13:53,700
into their personal billboard.

354
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:55,259
If you have no marketing budget,

355
00:13:55,259 --> 00:13:57,620
look for where your customers are already congregating,

356
00:13:57,620 --> 00:14:00,740
and build an ethical bridge to siphon them over.

357
00:14:00,779 --> 00:14:03,659
Second, the answer is always in your user's kitchen,

358
00:14:03,659 --> 00:14:04,620
not in your code.

359
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:07,740
When their revenue was flatlined at $200 a week,

360
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:09,419
they didn't rewrite their algorithm

361
00:14:09,419 --> 00:14:10,940
or redesign their logo.

362
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:12,820
They flew to Brooklyn, knocked on doors

363
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:15,419
with a rented camera, drank tea with their hosts,

364
00:14:15,419 --> 00:14:18,299
and fixed the real friction, terrible photos.

365
00:14:18,299 --> 00:14:20,220
In the early days of any business,

366
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:22,379
manual, unscalable legwork,

367
00:14:22,379 --> 00:14:24,659
we'll teach you more in 48 hours,

368
00:14:24,659 --> 00:14:27,220
than six months of staring at Google Analytics.

369
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:30,500
And third, never optimize the soul out of your product.

370
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:33,419
Airbnb's genius was that it was cheaper, warmer,

371
00:14:33,419 --> 00:14:34,940
and more personal than hotel.

372
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:37,740
But as soon as Wall Street demanded infinite growth,

373
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:40,700
they allowed corporate hosts to charge $400 bait ants,

374
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:43,940
which fees, and hand guests laminated shore lists.

375
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:46,899
The moment your customer feels like they are being squeezed

376
00:14:46,899 --> 00:14:49,820
by a spreadsheet instead of served by a human.

377
00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:52,139
You create the exact crack your competitors will use

378
00:14:52,139 --> 00:14:53,059
to destroy you.

379
00:14:53,059 --> 00:14:56,139
Brian Cheskey didn't build a $100 billion empire

380
00:14:56,139 --> 00:14:58,419
because he had a visionary master plan.

381
00:14:58,419 --> 00:15:00,940
He built it because he was resourceful enough

382
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:03,539
to turn an inflatable mattress into rent money,

383
00:15:03,539 --> 00:15:05,860
stubborn enough to sell novelty cereal

384
00:15:05,860 --> 00:15:07,539
to pay off credit card debt,

385
00:15:07,539 --> 00:15:09,779
and humble enough to knock on stranger's doors

386
00:15:09,779 --> 00:15:11,419
when his back was against the wall.

387
00:15:11,419 --> 00:15:12,820
You don't need a million dollars

388
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:14,899
in seed capital to start building today.

389
00:15:14,899 --> 00:15:17,259
You just need to find a real, urgent problem.

390
00:15:17,259 --> 00:15:19,259
Look at the tools you already have in front of you

391
00:15:19,259 --> 00:15:23,740
and do the unglamorous work that nobody else is willing to do.

392
00:15:23,740 --> 00:15:25,460
So the next time you sit there thinking

393
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:27,980
that every great business has already been built,

394
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:28,940
look around a room.

395
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:32,500
Every massive industry is hiding an annoying problem.

396
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:34,700
Someone is desperately waiting for you to solve.

397
00:15:34,700 --> 00:15:36,740
And if you can't find one sitting at your desk,

398
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:38,220
pack a bag and travel.

399
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:40,779
Step outside your routine, notice what breaks

400
00:15:40,779 --> 00:15:42,259
and see what frustrates people.

401
00:15:42,259 --> 00:15:45,100
It doesn't need to start with venture capital or a revolution.

402
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:47,980
It could start with an $80 air bed on a living room rug

403
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:49,860
and turn into $100 billion.

404
00:15:49,860 --> 00:15:51,299
What's in everyday annoyance?

405
00:15:51,299 --> 00:15:53,179
You think someone should turn into a business right now?

406
00:15:53,179 --> 00:15:54,740
Tell me in the comments down below.

407
00:15:54,740 --> 00:15:55,860
Stay curious.

408
00:15:55,860 --> 00:15:57,100
See you in the next one.
